.TH "RAPTOR-OPTIMISE" 1 "2026-10" "RAPTOR Studio" "RAPTOR Studio Manual"
.SH NAME
raptor\-optimise \- search spares, maintenance or crew sizes for a target
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBraptor optimise\fR [\-h] WHAT ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Search one kind of decision by simulation with paired trials: spare stock (the cheapest that meets a target), planned maintenance (whether and how often), or crew and tool sizes (the smallest that meets a target). A level meets a target only when its lower confidence bound does. The model file is only changed when \-\-apply is given.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBspares\fR
spare stock levels
.TP
\fBpm\fR
is planned maintenance worthwhile, and when
.TP
\fBresources\fR
how many crews or tools are needed
.SH RAPTOR OPTIMISE SPARES
\fBraptor optimise spares\fR [\-h] [\-\-target EXPR] [\-\-pools NAMES] [\-\-mode {meet,curve}] [\-\-budget N] [\-n N] [\-\-seed N|new] [\-j N] [\-\-timeout DURATION] [\-\-progress {auto,bar,json,none}] [\-\-apply] [\-f {text,json}] MODEL
.SS Options
.TP
\fB\-\-target\fR \fIEXPR\fR
e.g. system.ao>=0.995 (default)
.TP
\fB\-\-pools\fR \fINAMES\fR
comma list of pool names (default: every finite pool)
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fI{meet|curve}\fR
cheapest that meets (default) or the trade\-off curve
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fIN\fR
most simulation points to evaluate (default 40)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-trials\fR \fIN\fR
runs per point (default: the model's own)
.TP
\fB\-\-seed\fR \fIN|new\fR
salt every random stream (default: the fixed seed 'raptor run' uses, so the numbers match a plain run)
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-jobs\fR \fIN\fR
cores to run on (default 1). Any 2 or more give the same numbers; they differ, equally validly, from 1
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDURATION\fR
stop the study after DURATION, e.g. 90s, 10m (default 30m); what it measured by then is reported and marked partial
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR \fI{auto|bar|json|none}\fR
progress on stderr (auto: only on a terminal; json: one object per line; none, or \-q, for silence)
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
write the recommended stocks into the model
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI{text|json}\fR
output format (default text)
.SH RAPTOR OPTIMISE PM
\fBraptor optimise pm\fR [\-h] [\-\-blocks NAMES] [\-\-objective {availability,cost}] [\-n N] [\-\-seed N|new] [\-j N] [\-\-timeout DURATION] [\-\-progress {auto,bar,json,none}] [\-\-apply] [\-f {text,json}] MODEL
.SS Options
.TP
\fB\-\-blocks\fR \fINAMES\fR
comma list of block names (default: every block)
.TP
\fB\-\-objective\fR \fI{availability|cost}\fR
what to optimise (default availability)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-trials\fR \fIN\fR
runs per point (default: the model's own)
.TP
\fB\-\-seed\fR \fIN|new\fR
salt every random stream (default: the fixed seed 'raptor run' uses, so the numbers match a plain run)
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-jobs\fR \fIN\fR
cores to run on (default 1). Any 2 or more give the same numbers; they differ, equally validly, from 1
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDURATION\fR
stop the study after DURATION, e.g. 90s, 10m (default 30m); what it measured by then is reported and marked partial
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR \fI{auto|bar|json|none}\fR
progress on stderr (auto: only on a terminal; json: one object per line; none, or \-q, for silence)
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
write the recommended intervals into the model
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI{text|json}\fR
output format (default text)
.SH RAPTOR OPTIMISE RESOURCES
\fBraptor optimise resources\fR [\-h] [\-\-resources NAMES] [\-\-target EXPR] [\-\-extra N] [\-n N] [\-\-seed N|new] [\-j N] [\-\-timeout DURATION] [\-\-progress {auto,bar,json,none}] [\-\-apply] [\-f {text,json}] MODEL
.SS Options
.TP
\fB\-\-resources\fR \fINAMES\fR
comma list of resource names (default: all)
.TP
\fB\-\-target\fR \fIEXPR\fR
e.g. system.ao>=0.995 (default)
.TP
\fB\-\-extra\fR \fIN\fR
sizes above the seed to try (default 3)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-trials\fR \fIN\fR
runs per point (default: the model's own)
.TP
\fB\-\-seed\fR \fIN|new\fR
salt every random stream (default: the fixed seed 'raptor run' uses, so the numbers match a plain run)
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-jobs\fR \fIN\fR
cores to run on (default 1). Any 2 or more give the same numbers; they differ, equally validly, from 1
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDURATION\fR
stop the study after DURATION, e.g. 90s, 10m (default 30m); what it measured by then is reported and marked partial
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR \fI{auto|bar|json|none}\fR
progress on stderr (auto: only on a terminal; json: one object per line; none, or \-q, for silence)
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
write the recommended sizes into the model
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI{text|json}\fR
output format (default text)
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The cheapest spare stock whose lower bound meets 99.5 %.
.RS
.nf
\fBraptor optimise spares plant.rbds \-\-target 'system.ao>=0.995'\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every stock level evaluated, for a trade\-off chart.
.RS
.nf
\fBraptor optimise spares plant.rbds \-\-mode curve \-f json\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Whether planned maintenance pays on two pumps, and how often.
.RS
.nf
\fBraptor optimise pm plant.rbds \-\-blocks 'Pump A,Pump B'\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
The smallest crew that meets the target, written into the model.
.RS
.nf
\fBraptor optimise resources plant.rbds \-\-resources Fitters \-\-apply\fR
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBraptor\fR(1)
